2018-C-11127/GRC-2018-C-11127~medium.jpg","rel":"alternate","render":"image","width":1280,"size":171000,"height":854},{"href":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/GRC-2018-C-11127/GRC-2018-C-11127~small.jpg","rel":"alternate","render":"image","width":640,"size":44000,"height":427},{"href":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/GRC-2018-C-11127/GRC-2018-C-11127~thumb.jpg","rel":"preview","render":"image","width":640,"size":44000,"height":427},{"href":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/GRC-2018-C-11127/GRC-2018-C-11127~large.jpg","rel":"alternate","render":"image","width":1920,"size":381000,"height":1281},{"href":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/GRC-2018-C-11127/GRC-2018-C-11127~orig.jpg","rel":"canonical","render":"image","height":4912,"width":7360,"size":26214400}]},{"href":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/GRC-1973-C-00411/collection.json","data":[{"center":"GRC","date_created":"1973-01-23T00:00:00Z","description":"Technicians at work in the Materials Processing Laboratory’s Creep Facility at the National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) Lewis Research Center. The technicians supported the engineers’ studies of refractory materials, metals, and advanced superalloys. The Materials Processing Laboratory contained laboratories and test areas equipped to prepare and develop these metals and materials. The ultra-high vacuum lab, seen in this photograph, contained creep and tensile test equipment.      Creep testing is used to study a material’s ability to withstand long durations under constant pressure and temperatures. The equipment measured the strain over a long period of time. Tensile test equipment subjects the test material to strain until the material fails. The two tests were used to determine the strength and durability of different materials.    The Materials Processing Laboratory also housed arc and electron beam melting furnaces, a hydraulic vertical extrusion press, compaction and forging equipment, and rolling mills and swagers. There were cryogenic and gas storage facilities and mechanical and oil diffusion vacuum pumps. The facility contained both instrumental and analytical chemistry laboratories for work on radioactive or toxic materials and the only shop to machine toxic materials in the Midwest.","media_type":"image","nasa_id":"GRC-1973-C-00411","title":"High Vacuum Creep Facility in the Materials Processing Laboratory"}],"links":[{"href":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/GRC-1973-C-00411/GRC-1973-C-00411~medium.jpg","rel":"alternate","render":"image","width":1280,"size":212000,"height":960},{"href":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/GRC-1973-C-00411/GRC-1973-C-00411~small.jpg","rel":"alternate","render":"image","width":640,"size":69000,"height":480},{"href":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/GRC-1973-C-00411/GRC-1973-C-00411~thumb.jpg","rel":"preview","render":"image","width":640,"size":69000,"height":480},{"href":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/GRC-1973-C-00411/GRC-1973-C-00411~orig.jpg","rel":"canonical","render":"image"}]},{"href":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/iss073e0071487/collection.json","data":[{"center":"JSC","date_created":"2025-05-15T00:00:00Z","description":"iss073e0071487 (May 15, 2025) --- NASA astronaut and Expedition 73 Flight Engineer Nichole Ayers swaps sample cartridges inside the Material Science Laboratory (MSL) that supports high temperature space physics research using furnaces aboard the International Space Station's Destiny laboratory module. The properties of many types of materials such as metals, alloys, polymers, semiconductors, ceramics, crystals, and glasses, can be studied in the MSL to discover new applications for existing materials and new or improved materials.","description_508":"738T4771.NEF","media_type":"image","nasa_id":"iss073e0071487","title":"NASA astronaut Nichole Ayers swaps sample cartridges inside the Material Science Laboratory","album":["Expedition_73"]}],"links":[{"href":"https://images-assets.nasa.gov/image/iss0